      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\,i+(y-2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14129144/assets/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)",
